---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import Solids from '@/components/tools/calc2/Solids.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="volume-calculator">
  <Solids client:load measure="volume" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the volume calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      Pick a shape, enter its dimensions in one unit, and the volume calculator gives the volume in that
      unit cubed, with litres, US gallons and cubic metres next to it.
    </p>
    <pre><code>cube                V = a³
box                 V = l × w × h
cylinder            V = π r² h
cone                V = ⅓ π r² h
sphere              V = 4⁄3 π r³
hemisphere          V = 2⁄3 π r³
square pyramid      V = ⅓ a² h
triangular prism    V = T × length, T from Heron's formula
capsule             V = π r² a + 4⁄3 π r³</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A cone with a radius of 3 cm and a height of 4 cm holds π × 3² × 4 ÷ 3 = 37.699 cm³, which is
      0.0377 litres. A cylinder with the same base and height holds three times as much, 113.097 cm³.
    </p>

    <h2>Units</h2>
    <p>
      1 litre is 1,000 cm³; 1 m³ is 1,000 litres; 1 US gallon is 231 in³ (3.785 litres); 1 ft³ is
      7.48 US gallons.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The shapes are ideal: the walls have no thickness and the cone and pyramid are right (the tip is
      over the centre of the base). For a container, measure the inside dimensions.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
